  <page>
    <title>Madisonville, Kentucky</title>
    <id>115165</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41987779</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-03T01:41:26Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Kinneyboy90</username>
        <id>273946</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* History */ new story on murdered man</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Madisonville''' is a [[city]] located in [[Hopkins County, Kentucky|Hopkins County]], [[Kentucky]] of the [http://www.uky.edu/KGS/coal/webgeoky/pages/regionwestern.html Western Coal Field region].  As of the [[2000]] census, the city had a total population of 19,307.  It is the [[county seat]] of [[Hopkins County, Kentucky|Hopkins County]]{{GR|6}}.

The city was named in honor of [[President of the United States|U.S. President]] [[James Madison]].

Madisonville is a commercial center of the region with shopping, dining, and entertainment. The Madisonville area is the home to a mall, two movie theaters, a few casual dining establishments, a community college, the typical assortment of fast-food establishments, and its own radio stations. More commercial interests are being developed.

Geographically, Madisonville is bisected laterally by the Pennyrile Parkway, a north-south corridor that connects [[Hopkinsville, Kentucky|Hopkinsville]] with [[Henderson, Kentucky|Henderson]] and feeds into 41A at Hopkinsville, leading to I-24 East.  The Western Kentucky Parkway is only a few miles south.  The Western Kentucky Parkway's westbound lanes feed into I-24 West; the eastbound lanes feed into I-64 East via the Purchase Parkway at Elizabethtown, KY.  The Western Kentucky Parkway also intersects I-65, which feeds into Bowling Green, KY to the south.  In addition to trucking routes, a railway runs through Madisonville as well.

Hopkins County is a home to industry. [http://www.geae.com General Electric Aircraft Engines], [http://www.autoliv.com Autoliv], Period Furniture (a division of KI Furniture), GlitterWrap, and [http://www.carhartt.com Carhartt] and others all have operations in the Madisonville area.

Madisonville and most of Hopkins County is in the Hopkins County School District. Hopkins County Schools contains eight elementary school, four middle school, and two high school sub-districts. 

Dawson Springs is under the Dawson Springs Independent School District. Madisonville also has a Community College and Technical School and is within a short drive of several four year colleges and universities in Kentucky, Indiana, and Tennessee.

A recent tornado destroyed parts of the city, see History heading for more information.

== Geography ==
[[Image:KYMap-doton-Madisonville.PNG|right|Location of Madisonville, Kentucky]]
Madisonville is located at 37&amp;deg;19'58&quot; North, 87&amp;deg;30'8&quot; West (37.332660, -87.502190){{GR|1}}, some 125 miles southwest of [[Louisville, Kentucky]].

According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the city has a total area of 48.0 [[square kilometer|km&amp;sup2;]] (18.5 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  46.1 km&amp;sup2; (17.8 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 1.9 km&amp;sup2; (0.7 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is water.  The total area is 3.94% water.

It is at an altitude of 460 feet above [[sea level]].

The recent tornado did not effect the geography much, but even so, had some effect to some extent.

== Demographics ==
As of the [[census]]{{GR|2}} of [[2000]], there are 19,307 people, 8,077 households, and 5,330 families residing in the city.  The [[population density]] is 418.8/km&amp;sup2; (1,085.0/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 8,889 housing units at an average density of 192.8/km&amp;sup2; (499.5/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the city is 86.21% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 11.24% [[African American (U.S. Census)|Black]] or [[Race (U.S. Census)|African American]], 0.18% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 0.51% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.03% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 0.63% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 1.20% from two or more races.  1.34% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 8,077 households out of which 30.0% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 48.8% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 14.2% have a female householder with no husband present, and 34.0% are non-families. 30.4% of all households are made up of individuals and 13.3% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.31 and the average family size is 2.87.

In the city the population is spread out with 23.9% under the age of 18, 8.3% from 18 to 24, 27.4% from 25 to 44, 23.6% from 45 to 64, and 16.8% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 39 years.  For every 100 females there are 85.9 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 81.7 males.

The median income for a household in the city is $31,097, and the median income for a family is $38,688. Males have a median income of $32,064 versus $20,940 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the city is $19,381.  16.4% of the population and 13.0% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 27.5% of those under the age of 18 and 7.8% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

==Government==

Madisonville is the county seat of the Hopkins County area. The incumbent mayor is Karen Cunningham (Since late 1990's).

==History==

Madisonville was founded in 1808. 

On [[15 November]] [[2005]] a tornado ripped through the city, destroying some parts of it, and some others were less affected. The tornado began to form around 3 o'clock PM. The electricity in some parts was down until 7 PM, and in others until 9:00. The local Country Club neighborhood was greatly affected by the storm.  The Country Club's Clubhouse remained intact, however. Madisonville resident and former [[Boston Celtics]] star, [[Frank Ramsey (basketball)|Frank Ramsey's]] home was destroyed in the storm; fortunately, he was spared.  The mayor, Karen Cunningham instated a curfew in parts of the city and declared a state of emergency.

The city is relatively safe, but one incident in 2003 put a dent in that statement. A man named [[Russel Earl Winstead]] violently killed his 85 year old aunt Ann Branson — one of the city's most wealthy and privileged businesswoman. Winstead later appeared on [[Fox Broadcasting Company|Fox's]] [[America's Most Wanted]]. In 2005 he was captured in [[Costa Rica]]. He is set for trial and it's probable he will be convicted for murder. There was also a very recent story in which a former pharmacist who was likely involved in a drug ring was murdered — either as a result of, or by fire — as well. The story, as of 2 March [[2006]], is still developing.

{{Kentucky}}

